## 1. The strongest development: structure → projection → information loss

The strongest result in this batch is the **structure-first principle**.

The proposal observes repeatedly:

$$
E_t\rightarrow K_t
$$

$$
\mathcal B_t\rightarrow Gap
$$

$$
AF_t\rightarrow Balanced
$$

$$
ZeroLens\rightarrow DetectGap
$$

and then notices that the smaller representation is repeatedly being asked to answer questions whose information was discarded by the projection. 

That is mathematically legitimate.

For a map

$$
\pi:X\rightarrow Y
$$

the relation

$$
x_1\sim_\pi x_2
\iff
\pi(x_1)=\pi(x_2)
$$

describes exactly which distinctions the representation identifies. 

This gives us a much stronger formulation than “information is lost”:

$$
\boxed{
\text{A projection determines which distinctions become invisible downstream.}
}
$$

And therefore I agree with the correction already made in the corpus:

> **Every projection should declare the distinctions it identifies or discards.**

Not “every projection has a kernel” in the algebraic sense. For arbitrary semantic maps, the induced equivalence relation/fibres/quotient language is more rigorous. 

### This is potentially foundational.

I would currently rank this among the strongest mathematical candidates in the entire September 2 corpus.

