# 3. A very important refinement: invariant preservation

The most promising mathematical question emerging from this work is actually deeper than projection itself:

$$
\boxed{
\text{What must a representation preserve?}
}
$$

Suppose

$$
R_1\xrightarrow{\pi}R_2.
$$

Let

$$
\mathcal I=\{I_1,\ldots,I_n\}
$$

be the invariants required for a particular inquiry.

Then the meaningful question is whether

$$
Preserved(\pi,I)
$$

holds for every required invariant.

The corpus explicitly connects this with **invariant custody**: removing an operator is not free if the reduction causes an invariant to lose its custodian. 

This is considerably more promising than counting operators.

The research order therefore becomes:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Semantic Carrier}
\rightarrow
\text{Required Observables}
\rightarrow
\text{Semantic Equivalence}
\rightarrow
\text{Invariant Preservation}
\rightarrow
\text{Reduction}
\rightarrow
\text{Minimality}
}
$$



I strongly support this **as a research hypothesis/framework**, not yet as canonical theory.

