# 4. The multiplicity experiment produced a genuinely important statistical result

The “Linga's Millions” experiment becomes much more interesting once the biological metaphor is stripped away.

The experiment found:

$$
|\mathcal H|\uparrow
$$

does **not** imply better epistemic quality.

In the null world, apparent training quality increased from approximately \(0\) to \(0.61\), while genuine held-out quality stayed around zero. This is selection optimism / winner's curse. 

More importantly:

$$
\boxed{
\text{candidate multiplicity}
\leftrightarrow
\text{evidence burden}
}
$$

At fixed evidence, increasing the number of candidates can make a genuine signal inadmissible. The reported scaling is approximately

$$
m\propto 2\ln n.
$$



That is a legitimate statistical observation.

But the reviewer makes an important correction:

> “The fix is evidence, not thresholds”

is too absolute.

Multiplicity can be handled through combinations of evidence, multiplicity correction, structural restrictions, hierarchical modelling, replication, independent evidence, etc. 

So the safer research statement is:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Multiplicity control cannot be obtained for free.}
}
$$

