# 5. Count is not the same thing as effective complexity

This is another excellent development.

The corpus explicitly discovered:

$$
|\mathcal H|\neq Diversity(\mathcal H).
$$

A generator can produce 1,000 candidates while those candidates represent only a much smaller number of independent alternatives. 

That led to the candidate:

$$
N_{\mathrm{eff}}(\mathcal H,E,M,S).
$$

But then something very important happened: the attempted formalizations of \(N_{\mathrm{eff}}\) failed.

The frozen result says that neither the quotient construction nor δ-packing adequately captures joint statistical dependence. In particular, strong correlation can produce enormous discrepancies between pairwise distinguishability and actual family-level behaviour. 

This is excellent research discipline.

The question has therefore correctly changed from:

> “What formula is \(N_{\mathrm{eff}}\)?”

to:

$$
\boxed{
\text{What joint structure is required to represent family-level epistemic dependence?}
}
$$



That is a much better mathematical question.

