# 6. The decoy experiment is perhaps even more important

The experiment constructed:

$$
H_{\text{decoy}}
$$

with stronger apparent evidence than the true hypothesis:

$$
signal(H_{\text{decoy}})=0.9
$$

versus

$$
signal(H_{\text{true}})=0.3.
$$

Independent held-out validation nevertheless validated the decoy with approximately \(0.9849\). 

So:

$$
\boxed{
Selection\neq Validation
}
$$

is actually too weak.

The stronger result is:

$$
\boxed{
Agreement(Selection,Validation)\not\Rightarrow Truth
}
$$

when both rely on the same misleading evidence channel. 

This points toward an important distinction:

$$
\text{Evidence quality}
\neq
\text{Evidence quantity}
\neq
\text{Validation}
\neq
\text{Truth}.
$$

And potentially:

$$
\text{channel/measurement model}
$$

must itself become explicit.

That is a serious epistemic/statistical finding.

