# 10. Major warning: the Hilbert-space document is currently overclaiming

This is where my senior mathematician/statistician role becomes especially important.

The document at 12:23 suddenly claims that Hilbert space provides a “rigorous mathematical foundation” and says things such as:

$$
K_t\in\mathcal H
$$

$$
ZeroLens(K_t)=P_{\mathrm{Unknown}}(K_t)
$$

$$
P(H_i|E)=|\langle K_t,e_i\rangle|^2
$$

and

$$
K_1\equiv_{\mathrm{sem}}K_2
\iff
\|K_1-K_2\|<\varepsilon.
$$

 

**I do not accept these as established mathematics.**

There are several independent problems.

### A. Inner product does not generally mean statistical independence

The document says:

$$
\langle E_i,E_j\rangle
=
\text{independence measure}.
$$

That is not generally valid.

Orthogonality is a geometric property. Statistical independence is a probabilistic property. They coincide only under particular constructions and assumptions.

So:

$$
\boxed{
\langle E_i,E_j\rangle=0
\not\Rightarrow
E_i\perp E_j
\text{ statistically}
}
$$

without specifying the underlying probabilistic representation.

---

### B. Spectral decomposition is being used incorrectly

The document writes:

$$
K_t=\sum_i\lambda_i e_i
$$

and calls this a “spectral decomposition of knowledge.” 

A vector expansion in a basis is not automatically a **spectral decomposition**.

Spectral decomposition concerns operators under appropriate conditions.

You can have:

$$
K=\sum_i c_i e_i
$$

as a basis expansion without having a spectral theorem involved at all.

---

### C. “Unknown subspace” has simply been assumed

The definition

$$
ZeroLens(K)=P_{\mathrm{Unknown}}(K)
$$

requires that there already exists a mathematically defined decomposition:

$$
\mathcal H
=
\mathcal H_{\mathrm{known}}
\oplus
\mathcal H_{\mathrm{unknown}}.
$$

But the corpus has **not established such a decomposition**.

So the document has effectively solved Zero by introducing the mathematical object it needs.

That is exactly the projection-smuggling problem the structure-first research was designed to prevent.

---

### D. Norm ≠ confidence

The mapping

$$
\|K\|=\text{epistemic mass/confidence}
$$

is an interpretation, not a theorem.

Nothing about Hilbert-space axioms says that vector norm represents confidence.

---

### E. The proposed semantic equivalence is particularly problematic

The document proposes:

$$
K_1\equiv_{\mathrm{sem}}K_2
\iff
\|K_1-K_2\|<\varepsilon.
$$

But this presupposes:

1. a metric;
2. a meaningful embedding of semantic states into the same Hilbert space;
3. a justified \(\varepsilon\);
4. a proof that metric closeness preserves the relevant epistemic semantics.

None has been established.

It therefore cannot yet solve the semantic-equivalence problem.

