# 15. The most important insight emerging now

I think the September 2 corpus has unintentionally discovered something deeper than “KnowledgeOS should use projections.”

It is this:

$$
\boxed{
\textbf{Epistemic semantics may be fundamentally about preservation of distinctions.}
}
$$

A representation is not good because it contains “more information.”

It is adequate relative to an inquiry when it preserves the distinctions that the inquiry requires.

That suggests a candidate principle:

$$
\boxed{
Adequate(\pi,Q)
\Rightarrow
D_Q\subseteq Preserved(\pi)
}
$$

where \(D_Q\) is the set of distinctions required by inquiry \(Q\).

This is still **[PROP]**, not canonical.

But it potentially connects:

$$
\boxed{
\text{representation}
\rightarrow
\text{projection}
\rightarrow
\text{distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{adequacy}
\rightarrow
\text{reduction}
}
$$

and only **after that**:

$$
\rightarrow \text{kernel minimality}.
$$

That is a much more mathematically defensible path than trying to discover the kernel directly.

